1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,971
Lesson 0.1.

2
00:00:02,351 --> 00:00:04,035
AI's energy wall.

3
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:08,310
Artificial intelligence
is hitting a wall, and it

4
00:00:08,310 --> 00:00:11,018
is not a wall of algorithms but of energy.

5
00:00:11,398 --> 00:00:17,022
Today grid capacity is one of the main
obstacles to building data centers.

6
00:00:17,402 --> 00:00:20,828
In this first lesson we
answer a concrete question:

7
00:00:21,208 --> 00:00:23,793
where does a GPU's energy actually go?

8
00:00:24,173 --> 00:00:26,957
The answer will tell us
why it makes sense to look

9
00:00:26,957 --> 00:00:29,351
for a radically different
way of computing.

10
00:00:30,483 --> 00:00:34,907
Think of a very efficient factory whose
warehouse is on the other side of town.

11
00:00:35,287 --> 00:00:38,303
Making a part is cheap; what
is expensive is the truck that

12
00:00:38,303 --> 00:00:40,910
brings the raw material
and takes the product away.

13
00:00:41,290 --> 00:00:44,139
A modern chip works the
same way: the arithmetic

14
00:00:44,139 --> 00:00:46,987
core is the factory and
memory is the warehouse.

15
00:00:47,367 --> 00:00:50,242
Every datum travels along
wires, and every wire is a

16
00:00:50,242 --> 00:00:53,117
small capacitor that must
be charged and discharged.

17
00:00:53,497 --> 00:00:55,860
The longer the wire,
the more charge must be

18
00:00:55,860 --> 00:00:58,114
moved and the more energy is lost as heat.

19
00:00:59,217 --> 00:01:02,337
Let's see what happens when a
wire switches from zero to one.

20
00:01:02,717 --> 00:01:08,051
A voltage source V pushes charge
through a resistor into a capacitor C.

21
00:01:08,431 --> 00:01:13,079
The green curve shows the capacitor
voltage, which rises exponentially.

22
00:01:13,459 --> 00:01:16,732
The source delivers an
energy equal to C V².

23
00:01:17,112 --> 00:01:19,847
Half is stored in the
capacitor, and the other

24
00:01:19,847 --> 00:01:22,404
half is dissipated in
the resistor as heat.

25
00:01:22,784 --> 00:01:28,223
The surprise is that this half does not
depend on the resistance: a small resistor

26
00:01:28,223 --> 00:01:33,199
gives a large, brief current, a large
resistor gives a small, long current,

27
00:01:33,579 --> 00:01:36,413
but the area under the
power curve is the same.

28
00:01:36,793 --> 00:01:40,614
When the wire returns to zero, the
stored energy is dissipated too.

29
00:01:40,994 --> 00:01:43,498
Each full cycle costs C V².

30
00:01:44,617 --> 00:01:49,511
If a chip has a total switched
capacitance C, runs at voltage V and

31
00:01:49,511 --> 00:01:54,551
frequency f, and in each cycle only
a fraction α of the nodes switch,

32
00:01:54,931 --> 00:01:59,243
the dynamic power is P = α C V² f.

33
00:01:59,623 --> 00:02:01,868
Each factor has a physical meaning.

34
00:02:02,248 --> 00:02:06,460
Capacitance grows with the number
of transistors and with wire length.

35
00:02:06,840 --> 00:02:11,330
Voltage enters squared, which is why
lowering it was the most powerful lever.

36
00:02:11,710 --> 00:02:15,480
And frequency measures how many
times per second we pay that price.

37
00:02:16,583 --> 00:02:19,953
For decades, the industry
lived off Dennard scaling.

38
00:02:20,332 --> 00:02:25,089
If we shrink all dimensions of
a transistor by a factor k, its

39
00:02:25,089 --> 00:02:30,535
capacitance drops by k, voltage drops
by k and frequency can rise by k.

40
00:02:30,915 --> 00:02:34,542
Power per transistor then falls by k².

41
00:02:34,922 --> 00:02:39,906
But k² more transistors fit in
the same area, so power density

42
00:02:39,906 --> 00:02:44,649
stays constant: faster, denser
chips that don't run hotter.

43
00:02:45,029 --> 00:02:49,989
Around the mid-2000s, voltage stopped
dropping, because below a certain

44
00:02:49,989 --> 00:02:54,669
threshold leakage and thermal noise
make the transistor unreliable.

45
00:02:55,049 --> 00:02:58,489
With voltage fixed,
power density starts to

46
00:02:58,489 --> 00:03:01,688
grow as k², and clock frequencies stall.

47
00:03:02,817 --> 00:03:07,073
Let's run the numbers with typical
values, as an order-of-magnitude estimate.

48
00:03:07,453 --> 00:03:13,078
An on-chip wire has a capacitance
of about 0.2 fF per micrometer.

49
00:03:13,458 --> 00:03:17,557
A one-millimeter wire then has
about two hundred femtofarads.

50
00:03:17,937 --> 00:03:22,186
At 0.8 volts, one
transition dissipates half

51
00:03:22,186 --> 00:03:25,857
of C V², about sixty-four femtojoules.

52
00:03:26,237 --> 00:03:31,383
A local logic gate, at one femtofarad,
costs about two hundred times less.

53
00:03:31,763 --> 00:03:36,690
Classic measurements in 45-nanometer
technology confirm it: a

54
00:03:36,690 --> 00:03:41,213
32-bit floating-point add
costs less than one picojoule,

55
00:03:41,593 --> 00:03:45,126
but reading those 32 bits
from external DRAM costs

56
00:03:45,126 --> 00:03:48,307
on the order of six
hundred forty picojoules.

57
00:03:48,688 --> 00:03:52,033
Moving the datum costs hundreds of
times more than operating on it.

58
00:03:53,150 --> 00:03:56,017
In the von Neumann
architecture, memory and

59
00:03:56,017 --> 00:03:59,350
processor are separate and
communicate over a bus.

60
00:03:59,730 --> 00:04:02,350
The roofline model sums it up in one plot.

61
00:04:02,730 --> 00:04:06,111
The horizontal axis is
arithmetic intensity: how many

62
00:04:06,111 --> 00:04:09,365
operations we perform per
byte fetched from memory.

63
00:04:09,745 --> 00:04:13,037
Attainable performance
is the minimum of the

64
00:04:13,037 --> 00:04:16,254
compute peak and
bandwidth times intensity.

65
00:04:16,634 --> 00:04:19,511
Left of the knee, the
chip waits for memory.

66
00:04:19,891 --> 00:04:23,716
Batch-one decoding of a
language model lives there:

67
00:04:23,716 --> 00:04:27,542
each weight is read once
to do just two operations.

68
00:04:28,650 --> 00:04:30,345
Back to the opening question.

69
00:04:30,725 --> 00:04:34,788
In a GPU, much of the energy
goes into moving data between

70
00:04:34,788 --> 00:04:38,922
registers, on-chip memory,
external memory and the network.

71
00:04:39,302 --> 00:04:42,294
Another part goes into
guaranteeing voltage margins

72
00:04:42,294 --> 00:04:44,875
so every bit is
deterministic against noise.

73
00:04:45,255 --> 00:04:50,124
And when the algorithm is probabilistic,
like diffusion or token sampling, we

74
00:04:50,124 --> 00:04:54,677
also spend energy manufacturing
randomness with pseudorandom generators.

75
00:04:55,057 --> 00:04:57,873
In the lab one challenge
you will measure the

76
00:04:57,873 --> 00:05:00,439
energy per random sample on your own GPU.

77
00:05:01,550 --> 00:05:03,847
This lesson states the
thesis of the course.

78
00:05:04,227 --> 00:05:07,986
If randomness is so expensive
to manufacture and noise so

79
00:05:07,986 --> 00:05:11,613
expensive to suppress, why
not use thermal fluctuations

80
00:05:11,613 --> 00:05:15,438
directly to compute, and do
it locally, where the data is?

81
00:05:15,818 --> 00:05:20,061
In unit one we will see the
fundamental energy limit per bit, and

82
00:05:20,061 --> 00:05:24,239
in unit five we will learn to
separate the energy of the compute

83
00:05:24,239 --> 00:05:27,699
fabric, of input and output,
and of the digital glue.

84
00:05:28,817 --> 00:05:30,537
Four frequent confusions.

85
00:05:30,917 --> 00:05:33,891
Dissipation per transition
does not depend on

86
00:05:33,891 --> 00:05:36,668
the resistance: it is always half of C V².

87
00:05:37,048 --> 00:05:41,575
Counting operations is not enough,
because data movement often dominates.

88
00:05:41,955 --> 00:05:46,039
Moore's law and Dennard scaling
are not the same: density

89
00:05:46,039 --> 00:05:50,053
kept growing, but power per
area stopped being constant.

90
00:05:50,433 --> 00:05:54,228
And don't confuse power with
energy: the fair metric is

91
00:05:54,228 --> 00:05:57,402
energy per task, for
example joules per token.

92
00:05:58,517 --> 00:06:02,924
In summary: each node
transition costs C V²

93
00:06:02,924 --> 00:06:07,126
per cycle, and dynamic power is α C V² f.

94
00:06:07,506 --> 00:06:13,342
The end of Dennard scaling made power,
not transistors, the scarce resource.

95
00:06:13,722 --> 00:06:17,302
Moving data costs hundreds of
times more than operating on it,

96
00:06:17,302 --> 00:06:20,363
and memory separated from
compute limits performance.

97
00:06:20,743 --> 00:06:24,641
That is the energy wall that thermodynamic
computing tries to get around.

98
00:06:25,750 --> 00:06:28,259
Check your understanding
with four questions.

99
00:06:28,639 --> 00:06:30,885
Compute the heat
dissipated when charging a

100
00:06:30,885 --> 00:06:33,079
capacitor through two different resistors.

101
00:06:33,459 --> 00:06:36,917
Apply Dennard scaling
with a factor of 1.4.

102
00:06:37,297 --> 00:06:39,528
Repeat the calculation with voltage fixed.

103
00:06:39,908 --> 00:06:42,975
And find the roofline knee
for a hundred-teraflop

104
00:06:42,975 --> 00:06:45,917
chip with one terabyte
per second of bandwidth.
